WHAT THE ABOVE COVERS

Two licences, split by what a thing is rather than by which folder it sits in.
This repository is about as much a writing project as a software one, and one
licence over both halves would have been the wrong fit for whichever half it
was not written for.

The MIT licence above covers the CODE. That is the build (build.py, buildlib/,
tests/), everything under templates/ and shared/, every tool's src/, styles.css
and body.html, the scripts at the root, and every README - including each
tool's own, which is meant to travel with the code it explains.

Take a module. That is what it is there for: nothing under tools/*/src/ has an
import to install, and `node --test` already runs those files outside a browser.
Keep this notice with it and you have met the whole of the condition.

The WORDS THE SITE PUBLISHES are CC BY 4.0 instead - the guides and legal pages
under pages/, the taglines, descriptions, how-to steps, privacy panels and FAQ
answers inside tools/*/tool.toml and config/site.toml, and every translation of
those under locales/. LICENSE-CONTENT holds that licence and says how to credit
it. Some files hold both kinds; the licence follows the kind, not the file.

One folder is under neither. tools/heic-to-jpg/vendor/ holds libheif, which
nobody here wrote; it ships with its own LICENSE file beside it, and that file
is the one that governs it.

One file is under neither, for the same reason. The two wordlists inside
tools/password-generator/src/wordlist.js are the Electronic Frontier
Foundation's diceware lists, published at https://www.eff.org/dice and used
unchanged under CC BY 3.0 US. The code around them is MIT like everything else;
the words are EFF's, are credited in that file's own header and in that tool's
README, and stay under their licence. Nothing else in this repository is.
